>> 21 Explain compound interest with a simple example.
[completed 6s run=cmwvpecgg008z07p9oe1e5hgv] **Compound interest** is interest that earns interest on itself — your money grows on the original amount *and* on everything it's already earned.

**Simple example: €1,000 at 5% per year, compounded yearly**

| Year | Interest earned | End balance |
|------|----------------|-------------|
| 1 | €50 (5% of €1,000) | €1,050 |
| 2 | €52.50 (5% of €1,050) | €1,102.50 |
| 3 | €55.13 | €1,157.63 |
| 10 | — | €1,628.89 |

Compare that to **simple interest** (interest only on the original €1,000): after 10 years you'd have just €1,500 instead of €1,628.89. The €128.89 difference is the interest earning its own interest — and the gap widens every year after.

**The key takeaway:** time matters more than the rate. The earlier money starts compounding, the more dramatic the effect — which is why even small amounts invested early can outgrow larger amounts invested later.

The same math works in reverse, which is worth knowing too: debt compounds against you the same way.
